  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.3.8": "Sp-ṭ 1.180: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Udukkhalabhaṇḍikanti udukkhalatthāya ānītaṁ dārubhaṇḍaṁ</i>, “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Udukkhalabhaṇḍika</i>: wooden goods brought for the sake of a mortar.” That <i lang='pi' translate='no'>akkamati</i> can mean “kick” can be seen from <a href='https://suttacentral.net/pli-tv-bu-vb-pj1/en/brahmali#10.20.10'>Bu Pj 1:10.20.10</a>: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>So bhikkhu akkamitvā pavattesi</i>, “The monk kicked her off.” ",
  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.3.9": "",
  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.3.10": "",
  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.3.11": "",
  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.4.1": "",
  "pli-tv-bu-vb-pj3:5.4.2": "“For a certain event” is not found in the Pali, but has been added to make the Pali idiom clearer. Sp 2.108: <i lang='pi' translate='no'>Kāle ārociteti yāgubhattādīsu yassa kassaci kāle ārocite</i>, “<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Kāle ārocite</i>: when the time is announced for whatever, such as rice porridge, a meal, etc.” ",
